  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Friday: high pressure amplifies over the \nregion. This will steer most of the moisture north around the \nregion, with some periods higher clouds spreading over the \nnorthern to northeast CWA Thursday and then a few more clouds \ndevelop in advance of next system. Aside from some afternoon \nbreeziness around the Upper Columbia Basin, with gusts near 15 \nmph, winds will be light. High temperatures trend to the mid-60s\nand mid-70s, with lows in the 30s and 40s.\n\nSaturday: A mostly dry cold front moves through the area, with \na westerly flow and limited moisture. Clouds will thicken over \nthe region starting Friday night into Saturday morning. Rain \nchances come to the Cascades, with the best chances over the \ncrest; but at least a slight chances extends as far east as \nLeavenworth and the Upper Methow Valley. Snow levels will be \nhigh enough to mean mainly minor snow potential across the \nhigher mountains. Winds increase throughout the region coming \nout of the west to southwest in the late morning and afternoon. \nSpeeds of 10-20 mph are forecast, with gusts of 25-35 mph except\nin the more sheltered valleys. Per the NBM, the probability of \npeak wind gusts of 30 mph or greater from the West Plains to the\nWateville Plateau and around the4 Blues to Palouse is around \n70-80%, with a 20-60% chance of 40 mph gusts, with the highest \nprobability for that around the Waterville Plateau, Upper \nColumbia Basin and near the Blue Mountains. Impacts such as \ntravel difficulties, especially for high-profile vehicles, \nblowing dust are possible. Winds start to subside in the late \nafternoon to evening. Morning lows will be in the upper 30s \nthrough 40s and highs will be in the 60s to low 70s.\n\nSunday to Wednesday: the Inland NW will be in a more progressive\npattern, with a cycles of weak shortwave ridges and shortwave \ntroughs. In this quick pattern, sometimes models don't handle \nthe details. However look for dry weather Sunday, then some low \nprecipitation chances developing Monday to Wednesday. The best \nchances will in the mountains, with late Monday the best chance \nat the present moment over the east third of WA and lower \nelevations of ID. Either way precipitation amounts look light. \nThe chance of 0.10 inches of rain are only around 10-15% in the \nmountains. Snow levels average near 5-7kft. Exceptions will be \nnear the Cascades Sunday with snow levels near 4-4.5kft and \naround Tuesday when they will be near 4.5kft. Overall any snow \nwould be in the higher mountains. Temperature trend cooler, \nwith highs in the mid-50s to lower 60s and lows in the 30s, with\nsome upper 20s possible in the sheltered valleys and outlying \nareas. /Solveig",
